There is a subtle bug: createHttpClient's interceptor on 401 calls `refreshOnce()` which uses `refreshSession` shared. Good. But `handleUnauthorized` is defined inside; `notifyUnauthorized` uses module `unauthorizedHandler` (set by main). For SSE 401 after refresh fails, call `notifyUnauthorized`; but `storage.clearTokens` happens? defaultRefresh doesn't clear; API interceptor handleUnauthorized clears. For SSE we should clear tokens too? main's handler `auth.clearSession()` clears. Good; setUnauthorizedHandler callback does clearSession. But `notifyUnauthorized` only calls handler; main handler clears. Fine.

Now rewrite `src/api/chat.ts`. Need to parse error body in onopen and retry 401. Let's write full file.

Design:

```ts
import { fetchEventSource } from '@microsoft/fetch-event-source'
import { ApiRequestError, apiErrorFromResponse, notifyUnauthorized, refreshSession, resolveBaseUrl } from './client'
...
async function openOnce(payload, handlers): Promise<void> {
  await fetchEventSource(url, {
    ...
    async onopen(response) {
      if (response.status === 401) throw apiErrorFromResponse(401, await readJson(response))
      if (!response.ok) ...
      const ct...
      if (!ct.includes('text/event-stream')) throw new ApiRequestError(...)
      handlers.onOpen?.(...)
    },
    onmessage...
    onerror(error) {
      if (error instanceof ApiRequestError) throw error
      handlers.onError(...)
      throw error
    }
  })
}
export async function openChatStream(payload, handlers) {
  if mock...
  try { await openOnce } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof ApiRequestError && error.status===401) {
      try { await refreshSession(); await openOnce(...) }
      catch (retryError) { if retryError Api 401 -> notifyUnauthorized(); throw retryError }
      return
    }
    throw error
  }
}
```

Issue: fetchEventSource's `onerror` is called for onopen errors. If onopen throws ApiRequestError, onerror rethrows it; library likely rejects. Need confirm. Without node_modules, can't. But known implementation: 

```ts
try {
  ...
  const response = await fetch(input, fetchOptions);
  if (response.status === 204) return;
  if (!response.ok) { ... }
  await onopen(response);
  response.body.pipeThrough(...).pipeTo(...)
  ...
} catch (err) {
  if (curRequestController.signal.aborted) return;
  if (!onerror || !(await onerror(err))) { ...?}
}
```
Actually let me recall exact source from @microsoft/fetch-event-source v2.0.1:

```js
async function create() {
  curRequestController = new AbortController();
  try {
    const response = await fetch(input, {...});
    await onopen(response);
    ...
    const reader = response.body?.getReader();
    ...
  } catch (err) {
    if (!curRequestController.signal.aborted) {
      try {
        const interval = onerror?.(err) ?? retryInterval;
        window.clearTimeout(retryTimer);
        retryTimer = window.setTimeout(create, interval);
      } catch (innerErr) {
        dispose();
        reject(innerErr);
      }
    }
  }
}
```
If that's the case, onerror throws -> reject(innerErr) with our ApiRequestError. Good. If onerror returns (doesn't throw), it retries. We throw.

But if `onopen` throws and `onerror` throws, reject with error. Great.

Also note `onerror` will be called for network errors; we call handlers.onError then throw Error, causing rejection. But we may want automatic reconnect? No, store handles retry manually; fine. However `handlers.onError` sets state error; then outer catch also handles, duplicate. We'll adjust store to not double set. In onerror, for non-API errors, call handlers.onError? Could skip and let outer catch handle; but `handlers.onError` is part of StreamHandlers interface and mock uses. Keep call; store's onError can set error; catch sees same error and if state.error already set, okay. But for ApiRequestError, don't call.

`readJson`:
```ts
async function readErrorBody(response: Response): Promise<unknown> {
  try { return await response.json() } catch { try { return await response.text() } catch { return null } }
}
```
Cannot read body twice; fine.

`X-Run-ID` header on error absent; no.

Use `credentials`? Bearer; not needed.

`openWhenHidden: true` already.

Also `Content-Type` for error may be application/json; check.

Let's write.

